h of Kellogg 's <ei28 erosion> has been in such core brands as Corn Flakes , Rice Krispies and Frosted Flakes , which represent nearly one-third of its sales volume . Kellogg is so <ei29 anxious> to <ei30 turn> around Corn Flakes sales that it soon will <ei32 begin> <ei33 selling> boxes for as little as 99 cents , trade sources <ei34 say> . \" Cheerios and Honey Nut Cheerios have <ei35 eaten> away sales normally <ei36 going> to Kellogg 's corn-based lines simply because they are <ei38 made> of oats , \" <ei39 says> Merrill Lynch food analyst William Maguire . \" They are not a <ei40 happy> group of people at Battle Creek right now . \" Kellogg is based in Battle Creek , Mich . , a city that calls itself the breakfast capital of the world . Another analyst , John C. Maxwell Jr. of Wheat , First Securities in Richmond , Va . , recently <ei43 went> to a \" sell \" recommendation on Kellogg stock , which <ei44 closed> Friday at $ 71.75 , down 75 cents , in New York Stock Exchange composite trading . \" I do n't <ei46 think> Kellogg can <ei47 get> back to 40 % this year , \" he <ei49 said> . \" Kellogg 's main problem is life style . People are <ei50 reading> the boxes and <ei51 deciding> they <ei52 want> something that 's ` healthy ' for you -- oats , bran . \" Mr. Maxwell <ei53 said> he would n't be <ei54 surprised> if , over the next two years or so , General Mills ' share <ei56 increased> to 30 % or more . In <ei57 announcing> the plant <ei58 delay> , Kellogg Chairman William E. LaMothe <ei59 said> , \" Cereal volume <ei60 growth> in the U.S. has not <ei61 met> our <ei62 expectations> for 1989 \" . He <ei64 said> <ei65 construction> would n't <ei66 resume> until market conditions <ei67 warrant> it . Kellogg <ei68 indicated> that it <ei69 has> room to <ei70 grow> without <ei71 adding> facilities . The company <ei72 has> five other U.S. plants , including a modern facility at its Battle Creek headquarters known as Building 100 , which is to <ei73 add> bran-processing and rice-processing capacity next year . General Mills , meanwhile , finds itself <ei75 constrained> from <ei76 boosting> sales further because its plants are <ei78 operating> at capacity . A large plant in Covington , Ga . , is to <ei79 come> on line next year . A Kellogg officer , who asked not to be named , <ei81 said> the Memphis project was \" <ei82 pulled> in for a <ei83 reconsideration> of costs , \" an indication that the ambitious plans might be <ei84 scaled> back in any future <ei85 construction> . Initial cost estimates for the plant , which was to have been built in phases , <ei86 ranged> from $ 1 billion to $ 1.2 billion . A company spokesman <ei87 said> it was \" possible , but highly unlikely , \" that the plant might never be <ei88 built> . \" As we <ei89 regain> our leadership level where we have been , and as we <ei90 continue> to <ei91 put> new products into the marketplace and <ei92 need> additional capacity , we will <ei93 look> at <ei94 resuming> our <ei95 involvement> with our plan , \" he <ei96 said> . The new facility was to have been the world 's most advanced cereal manufacturing plant , and Kellogg 's largest construction project . The company had <ei97 retained> the Fluor Daniel unit of Fluor Corp. as general contractor . But in recent weeks , construction-industry sources <ei99 reported> that early preparation work was <ei100 slowing> at the 185-acre site . Subcontractors <ei101 said> they were <ei102 told> that equipment <ei103 orders> would be <ei104 delayed> . Fluor Daniel already has <ei105 reassigned> most of its work crew , the sources <ei106 said> . Last Friday 's <ei108 announcement> was the first official word that the project was <ei109 in> trouble and that the company 's plans for a surge in market share may have been overly <ei110 optimistic> . Until recently , Kellogg had been <ei112 telling> its sales force and Wall Street that by 1992 it <ei114 intended> to <ei115 achieve> a 50 % share of market , measured in dollar volume